# 5. Implementation

**Draft.** This chapter condenses `hls-doc/architecture.md` and the pass pages in `hls-doc/passes/`;
those pages hold the details, options and worked examples. Code: `~/circt` branch
`hls-est-vitis-match` ({{circt_commits}} commits on top of `main` at `{{circt_head}}`) and Polygeist
branch `hls-est-vitis-match` ({{polygeist_commits}} commits on top of `main` at `{{polygeist_head}}`).

## 5.1 Overview

hls-est turns a C/C++ HLS kernel into a per-memory BRAM_18K estimate in three steps, each a separate
program:

```
kernel.cpp + #pragma HLS
   | cgeist (Polygeist): clang front end with the real Vitis headers, pragmas -> hls.* attributes,
   |                     Vitis-like front-end behaviours, loops raised to the affine dialect
   v
kernel.mlir  (func / affine / memref / arith / scf / math / llvm ops with hls.* attributes)
   | hls-est: an mlir-opt style driver running a {{pipeline_passes}}-pass pipeline,
   |          configured by a JSON document and plugins
   v
one block per memory (kind, banks, copies, BRAM_18K), m_axi adapters, math cores, "Total BRAM: N"
   + an optional debug report (text or JSON) that explains every number
   | harness (tools/hls-est-validate): joins the estimate with the Vitis ground truth per array
   v
per-design and per-array comparison, workbook, results pages, lit regression tests
```

The order of hls-est's stages follows the order in which Vitis takes the corresponding decisions,
established from the intermediate files Vitis writes (N1a, N9): arrays are deleted and folded before
`a.o.1.ll`; pipelining, unrolling and automatic partitioning are decided before `a.o.1.ll`; widths are
narrowed between `a.o.1.ll` and `a.o.2.ll`; scheduling, binding and the RAM types come after. Each hls-est
stage must see the IR its Vitis counterpart sees, and a stage placed too early can break a later decision
(an early loop-invariant code motion, for example, makes perfect loop nests look imperfect and changes the
flatten decision).

## 5.2 The front end: cgeist with the real Vitis headers

cgeist is the C/C++ front end of Polygeist [@moses2021polygeist], extended for HLS. The HLS-specific
code is in {{files_cgeist_hls}} files (`tools/cgeist/Lib/*HLS*`, `*Xlx*`, {{loc_cgeist_hls}} lines). It
does four things hls-est depends on.

**Pragmas.** `--hls-annotate` turns every `#pragma HLS` into an `hls.*` attribute on the function,
parameter, loop, local or global it applies to (`hls.pipeline`, `hls.unroll`, `hls.array_partition`,
`hls.bind_storage`, `hls.interface`, `hls.stream`, `hls.dataflow`, `hls.inline_off`, ...). The contract
between cgeist and hls-est is written down in Polygeist's `docs/hls-pragmas.md`; what each pragma becomes
is in `hls-doc/frontend-pragmas.md`.

**Vitis' front-end behaviours.** Where Vitis' front end makes a decision that changes the IR hls-est sees,
cgeist copies it: two `array_partition` pragmas on one dimension are merged the Vitis way (the larger
factor wins, `complete` wins; N24, N28); functions Vitis keeps as calls stay calls, by Vitis' automatic
inlining rule (N18) and by `inline off` (N14); a loop whose counter is narrower than a run-time bound is
raised to an affine loop and marked `hls.uncounted` (N15).

**The real Vitis headers.** Originally cgeist compiled designs against hand-written stand-ins ("shims")
of `ap_int.h`, `ap_fixed.h` and `hls_stream.h`. Because every header change required a shim change
(MEMOS #39), cgeist now compiles the headers of the Vitis {{vitis_versions}} installation the way the
Vitis front end does: a patched clang reads `__attribute__((bitwidth(N)))` as an exact N-bit integer, the
Xilinx bit, part-select and concatenation builtins and the `__fpga_*` intrinsics are lowered, `hls_math.h`
calls are marked as math cores, and `ap_int` / `ap_fixed` objects are passed to hls-est as plain `iN`
integers (MEMOS #66, #67; the shims remain selectable for comparisons). Some of this code was ported from
AMD's Apache-2.0 front-end files with their notices kept (MEMOS #40).

**Loops and memories.** `--raise-scf-to-affine` raises loops to `affine.for` where it can and
`--memref-fullrank` keeps arrays as full-rank memrefs; every local carries its C name
(`polygeist.varname`), which the harness uses to match arrays with Vitis' memory table.

**Semantic validation.** An estimate is only meaningful if the MLIR computes what the C program computes.
`check_semantics.py` lowers each design's MLIR to LLVM, runs it on seeded inputs and compares every output
buffer bit for bit with a native build against the real Vitis headers (`hls-doc/semantics.md`). Of the
{{sem_records}} designs with a record, {{sem_equal}} are bit-exact, {{sem_mismatch}} mismatch and
{{sem_na}} cannot be checked (for most of them the native reference itself is defective: uninitialised
reads, out-of-bounds accesses, missing third-party headers; `hls-doc/done-exclusions.md` lists
{{dod_defective_refs}} such designs and the {{dod_vitis_rejects}} designs whose source Vitis itself rejects).

## 5.3 Data model: memrefs and attributes

A memory is a memref: a `memref.alloca` (a local array), a `memref.alloc`, or a `memref.global` with all
its `memref.get_global` uses (a file-scope or `static` array). hls-est attaches a
`MemoryResourceOpInterface` (element type, shape, size in bits, storage pragma, partition) to allocas and
globals as an external model. After banking, every bank is a memref of its own, linked to its array by
`hls.bank_of`. The memref arguments of the top function are the interface ports; they are priced only
through the m_axi adapter and interface rules.

All decisions travel as attributes in the IR: the pass that makes a decision writes an `hls.*` attribute
and the passes that use it read the attribute; no pass reads another pass's in-memory state. This makes
every intermediate IR inspectable (the Compiler Explorer integration in `tools/hls-est-ce/` shows the IR
after every pass) and every pass testable in isolation with a small MLIR input. Schedules use CIRCT's
LoopSchedule dialect (`loopschedule.pipeline` with II, trip count and stages).

## 5.4 The pass pipeline

The default pipeline is listed in Table `tables/frame-pipeline.md` ({{pipeline_passes}} passes, of which
{{pipeline_own_passes}} were written or modified for this work; {{pass_pages}} pass pages in
`hls-doc/passes/`). The harness prepends MLIR's `--symbol-privatize` / `--symbol-dce` to keep only the
top function and what it calls. The passes form five blocks.

1. **Front block** (Vitis' front end and the first reflow stage): `--lower-polygeist-subindex` removes
   cgeist's row and offset views; `--inline` inlines every call Vitis does not keep;
   `--split-struct-memrefs` gives every struct member its own memory, or one packed memory under an
   `aggregate` pragma (N29, N37); `--mark-constant-fills` marks arrays written only with constants, which
   become ROMs whose width comes from their values (N26); MLIR's `--canonicalize` and `--sccp`;
   `--vitis-fold` reproduces Vitis' folding of integer division by zero to its undefined value (N13); and
   `--dead-memory-elimination` removes arrays whose values reach no output, seeded from the top function's
   outputs (N1, N1a, N66).
2. **Loop structure**: `--affine-loop-normalize` and `--auto-pipeline-unroll`, Vitis' automatic
   pipeline / unroll / flatten decision (N3, with the trip-count-1 and flatten-refusal cases of N40, N64,
   N76).
3. **Memory structure**: `--infer-vitis-split-array=td=off` adds the automatic partitions that remain
   with throughput-driven partitioning off (small arrays, constant indices: N7, N43, N57, N61, N77);
   `--narrow-ram-widths` narrows a RAM's element width to the bits its stored values need and its loads
   demand (N35, N41); `--memory-banking-bram` splits partitioned arrays into banks and rewrites every
   access to its bank, with predicated stores (`hls.store_enable`) where the bank is only known at run time.
4. **Schedule**: `--convert-affine-to-loopschedule` performs Vitis' middle-end clean-ups (invariant-load
   hoisting, load CSE, store-to-load forwarding: N42, N70, N73) and then modulo-schedules every pipelined
   loop with per-bank port limits. It is a modified copy of CIRCT's `AffineToLoopSchedule`; the schedulers
   are CIRCT's simplex scheduler and a greedy resource-bound modulo scheduler written for large flattened
   bodies, selected by body size.
5. **Estimate**: `--bram-analysis` prices every memory (Section 5.5).

## 5.5 Inside `--bram-analysis`

`--bram-analysis` decides what each memref is and what its accesses demand, and asks the configured
plugins how it is built and what it costs.

- **What is it?** Registers (fully partitioned), excluded types, dead or zero ROMs cost nothing; an
  `hls::stream` is a FIFO (shift registers when shallow or small, else RAMB18 bands at x18; N31, N49,
  N58, N67, N72); a dataflow channel is a ping-pong buffer (PIPO), doubled exactly when a second process
  drives a port of it (N33, N36, N48, N84).
- **Storage tier.** Words x narrowed bits below 1024 goes to distributed (LUT) RAM and costs no BRAM (N5).
- **Port demand.** For every access, through calls, the analysis finds its scope: in a pipeline, the
  stage modulo the II; in sequential code, straight-line stretches where two independent accesses share a
  state (N12). The port demand of a bank is the maximum number of accesses in one cycle.
- **Memory architecture.** A bank read more often than its two ports allow in a pipelined loop is
  replicated as `RAM_1WNR` with a copy count from reads, writes and II (N4, N38), unless it is
  port-bound (a written global, a parameter bound to several arrays: N75, N85); the policy also chooses
  the storage kind (`RAM_1P`, `RAM_2P`, `RAM_T2P`, ROM) and the priced port configuration (simple
  dual-port allows x36, true dual-port at most x18).
- **Mapping.** The number of RAMB18 per copy follows the 7-series depth bands and aspect modes (N6, N8).
- **Instances.** A memory of a function Vitis keeps as a call is counted once per instance (N14, N59).
- **Implicit buffers and cores.** Each m_axi bundle adds an adapter with a read FIFO and a write buffer
  (N1a, N50, N56, N63), and each `hls::` math core instance adds the BRAM of its tables (N16, N45).

The scheduler and the estimate use the same memory-architecture plugin for the port pools, so the II the
scheduler finds and the copies the estimate prices come from one model.

## 5.6 Configuration and plugins

Every architectural assumption that is data is a key of one JSON configuration document; what cannot be
data is a plugin interface (MEMOS #37, #38). The default configuration is the compiled-in device
`amd-xc7z020clg400-1` with the tool profile `vitis-2025.1`; with it, hls-est's output is byte-identical
to the output before configuration existed.

- **Configuration.** {{config_keys}} keys (counted from `hls-doc/configuration.md`, which is generated
  from `hls-est --dump-config-schema`) in sections `run`, `device` (resource classes, memory primitives
  and their geometries, port configurations), `tool` (the HLS policies and the memory back end),
  `frontend` and `scheduling`. Files given with `--hls-config` and single keys given with `--config-set`
  override the defaults; a pass option on the command line overrides both. Unknown keys, wrong types and
  inconsistent combinations are errors that name the key and the file.
- **Plugins.** {{plugin_interfaces}} interfaces (Table `tables/frame-plugins.md`) with
  {{plugin_builtins}} builtin implementations: storage selector, memory architecture policy, memory
  mapper, memory grouping, implicit buffer model, pipeline policy, partition policy, fold rule set, and
  five scheduling interfaces (operator timing, dependences, modulo scheduler, sequential scheduler,
  binder). No interface names an AMD resource or a Vitis storage kind: costs are vectors over the device's
  resource classes and memories are device memory ids, so the Vitis rules are one tool-policy
  implementation among possible others. A separately compiled library is loaded with `--load-plugin`; an
  example plugin with its own build is exercised by the lit tests in `test/hls-est/Plugins/`.
- **A second architecture.** `hls-doc/new-architecture.md` retargets hls-est to Microchip PolarFire
  (LSRAM and uSRAM, each with a fabric overhead) with a new device file and one new storage-selector
  plugin. It is explicitly unvalidated: no Microchip tool was run, and the selection rules are designer
  knobs, not observations.

## 5.7 The debug report

`--bram-debug` (text) and `--bram-debug-json` (JSON, version 2) explain every number: per memref its
status and reason, provenance (C name, function, location), partitioning and its source, every access with
its scope, II and stage, the port demand per scope, the replication decision and its limiting loop, the RAM
type and reason, and the bank arithmetic (configured width, depth bands, blocks per copy); plus the
pipelines, call instances, folds and the configuration in effect. JSON changes are additive. Debug output
must never change an estimate: `check_debug_invariance.py` (also a build target) runs a set of designs with
and without every debug flag and compares the estimates, and one lit test compares the two outputs with
`diff`.

## 5.8 Harness and regression net

The harness (`tools/hls-est-validate/`, {{files_harness}} Python scripts, {{loc_harness}} lines) has four
parts.

- **Ground truth.** `run_vitis.py` synthesises every catalogue design once with Vitis through `cmd.exe`
  (Vitis runs on the Windows host, hls-est in WSL) and records totals, memory and loop tables and the
  transformation log in `results/vitis.json` ({{vitis_records}} records, {{vitis_ok}} successful).
  `vitis_adhoc.py` runs the minimal kernels of the inference experiments into a separate experiment cache,
  never into the ground truth.
- **Predictions and comparison.** `run_hlsest.py` runs cgeist (cached) and hls-est on every design under a
  memory cap; `compare.py` joins hls-est and Vitis per design and per array, matching arrays by their C
  name through the debug JSON. Generators render the workbook, the comparison CSVs and the results pages.
- **Regression net.** `check-hls-est` runs lit over `test/hls-est`: {{lit_passes}} hand-written pass tests,
  {{lit_estimation}} end-to-end estimation tests, configuration and plugin tests, and the generated Mirror
  suite: one hls-est test per cgeist HLS lit test ({{mirror_tests}} Mirror tests for {{cgeist_hls_tests}}
  cgeist tests; {{mirror_no_test}} cgeist tests cannot be compiled and are listed with their reason), whose
  CHECK lines pin hls-est's current output, with the Vitis number as a comment and never as a CHECK. A
  change that is meant to change estimates must name every CHECK it changes in the changelog.
- **Gates.** Every change must keep lit no worse, keep four PolyBench designs exact (bicg
  {{gate_bicg_vitis}}, gesummv {{gate_gesummv_vitis}}, mvt {{gate_mvt_vitis}}, syrk {{gate_syrk_vitis}}:
  {{gates_all_exact}} in the newest run) and keep every exact design exact unless an open issue explains
  the change per array.

Every change is recorded in `hls-doc/notes/CHANGELOG.md` with its measured effect ({{changelog_entries}}
entries from {{changelog_first}} to {{changelog_last}}), decisions in `MEMOS.md` ({{memos_decisions}}
numbered decisions) and open problems in `OPEN_ISSUES.md` ({{o_count}} issues, O1 to O{{o_last}}).

